µs/vector is per vector per repeat, from the FASTEST run. Everything else
sharing the machine can only ever add time, never subtract it, so the fastest
run is the least contaminated one. The spread beside it says how contaminated
the others were: a large spread means the machine was busy, not that one
implementation is erratic, and the run is worth repeating on a quiet one.
